# {{.Slug}}: Flare incident evidence draft

Generated {{.Date}}. This workflow turns a signed, project-bound `flare-v1`
notice into a small review draft. It checks the issue's project, requests fresh
triage, and confirms the exact succeeded AI receipt and evidence digest. Issue
text, stack traces, model output and member credentials stay out of Reactor's
step outputs. The authenticated run page shows the bounded draft and a link to
Flare for inspection.

## Configure and review

1. Set `trustedFlareBaseURL` to the reviewed HTTPS origin (no path, query or
   nonstandard port), and `trustedFlareProjectID` to the expected project ID in
   `main.go`. These are operator-owned source configuration; webhook input and
   model output cannot select them.
2. Store a Flare member credential in Reactor's vault as
   `flare-member-{{.Slug}}`. Grant only this workflow and its tenant access to
   that credential using the existing vault ACL. Never paste the bearer into
   source, CLI arguments, input, or a journaled step result. This version uses
   Flare's existing member authorization; its project check is an additional
   boundary, not a replacement for the credential's permissions.
3. Build locally with `reactor workflow build --src . --slug {{.Slug}}` and
   review the generated source, DAG and exact artifact before any explicit
   registration or activation. `reactor new` and this template do neither.
4. Provision a dedicated project-bound `flare-v1` trigger through the reviewed
   Reactor operator flow. Use its `/webhook/flare/{token}` URL and signing
   secret for the Flare automation channel. The generic webhook path does not
   accept this provider. See Reactor's `docs/flare-automation.md` for ingress
   configuration and delivery acceptance.

A signed channel `test` is a no-op. Notices without an issue do not call AI.
Manual workflow input is validated again against the same six-field schema and
project before any credential is resolved.

## Retry and review boundaries

`flare-fresh-triage` has one automatic attempt. Its successful output journals
only the fresh receipt ID and digest. `flare-confirm-draft` separately retries
pending receipt reads up to four attempts; cached, failed, foreign or mismatched
evidence cannot produce a draft. Completed replay reads both outputs without
fetching live vault credentials. The replay identity also binds the trusted
origin, project, notice and exact fresh receipt/digest.

An interruption after Flare accepted triage but before Reactor journaled the
result is ambiguous. Inspect Flare's receipt history before any manual redrive;
this workflow cannot promise exactly one external provider call across that
window. Never enable automatic redrive of the triage step.

The workflow stops at `status: draft`. A draft is not human approval, a patch,
a passed CI run, a merge, a deployment or a synthetic acceptance receipt. Those
later actions need a separate reviewed protocol and explicit human approval.
